<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jacinto Canek</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1731–<a href="/years/1761">1761</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jacinto Canek was a Maya leader who led an uprising against Spanish rule at Cisteil in Yucatán in November 1761. Taking the name of the last Itza kings, he was proclaimed king by his followers, but Spanish forces crushed the revolt within days. He was tortured and executed in Mérida in December 1761, and he is remembered as a symbol of Maya resistance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jacinto-canek.jpg" alt="Jacinto Canek" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Yodigo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacinto_Canek.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Jacinto Canek was born Jacinto Uc de los Santos in the barrio de San Román of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campeche">Campeche</a>, a little over thirty years after the fall of the Itza had completed Spanish control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán Peninsula</a>. He studied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and history at the Franciscan monastery of the Convento Grande in Mérida but was expelled for his rebellious spirit, and for some years he worked as a baker. Historians believe that his education and his expulsion fed his bitterness towards colonial rule and the Catholic church and his identification with the Maya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He reached Cisteil, a village near Sotuta, on 3 or 4 November 1761, quarrelled with the visiting parish priest of Tixcacaltuyub and threatened to kill him. By 12 November almost the whole village accepted him as leader. On 19 or 20 November he told an assembly in Maya that Spanish rule had brought nothing but painful servitude, tribute and whippings. On 19 November his men killed Diego Pacheco, a Spanish merchant who had come to collect debts, and Canek was crowned with the crown of the church's statue of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/our-lady-of-guadalupe">Virgin of Guadalupe</a>, claiming magical powers and the help of five brujos.</p><p>On 20 November Captain Tiburcio Cosgaya arrived to put down the rising and was killed in the skirmish with five of his soldiers; eight Maya also died. The governor called up the militia and ordered the Maya of the province disarmed. On 26 November a force of 500 soldiers under Estanislao del Puerto and Cristóbal Calderón met 1,500 Maya entrenched in the plaza of Cisteil and won in hand-to-hand fighting. The village was burned, and 500 Maya were said to have died in the blaze. Canek escaped to Huntulchac but was taken at Sibac with about 125 followers.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He was sentenced to be tortured, broken and burned, and the sentence was carried out in the main plaza of Mérida on 14 December 1761. Eight of his companions were hanged, and 200 other participants received 200 lashes and lost an ear. Governor José Crespo y Honorato blamed the rising on the Maya being allowed firearms for hunting, on their incomplete conversion and on tolerated festivals. When the Caste War broke out in 1847, Canek's name became a rallying cry.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jacinto Canek” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Campeche was its fortified port, often raided by pirates, and Maya revolts such as <b>Jacinto Canek's</b> in 1761 recurred.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-yucatan">Province of Yucatán</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in San Francisco de Campeche, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merida-yucatan">Mérida</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=19.85,-90.54&amp;year=1750" aria-label="San Francisco de Campeche on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2107 1429 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jacinto Canek"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2557 1699h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2557 1699h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=19.85,-90.54&amp;year=1750">What was here in 1750? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
